Cell["\<\
The final output contains, in order: the background QG components, the \
background dust components, the linear QG components, and the linear dust \
components.  Equating the first pair gives the dust-FLRW background \
equations; equating the second pair gives the complete linear scalar \
equations of motion.  This is the final result in the same grid-based xPand \
notation used by the supplied notebooks.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"cb6cfd65-605d-416d-b23d-6cdc01fe180d"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Conformally-flat, sub-horizon and Fourier reduction", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"8d80dc2d-9b2e-4584-800d-e4740e085a3a"],

Cell["\<\
This section reduces the complete xPand result above to the local scalar \
equations used in the presentation.  It is an additional approximation, not \
an identity for the full FLRW output: retain only the leading spatial \
derivatives, use the conformally-flat local background, and then Fourier \
transform.  The xPand Newton-gauge potentials are mapped as Phi_h -> Psi and \
Psi_h -> Phi.  Therefore the reduced metric is ds^2 = a(eta)^2[-(1+2 \
Psi)deta^2 + (1-2 Phi) delta_ij dx^i dx^j].\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a313a2b1-ad66-4d7a-a2c9-e1ea4e955fae"],

